\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{sports.core.api.espn.com/v2/sports/football/leagues/college-football/seasons/{year}/recruits}.
The season index is \verb{$ref}-paginated and large (roughly 3,700-4,900
recruits per class), so dereferencing every recruit would be thousands
of HTTP calls. ESPN exposes no pre-expanded recruiting endpoint -- the
\code{site.web.api.espn.com} recruiting paths all 404 -- so this wrapper caps
the walk at \code{max_results} recruits (default 100). Raise \code{max_results}
(e.g. to several hundred or higher) to pull more of the class, at the
cost of one extra HTTP call per recruit.

The index is returned by ESPN in athlete-name (alphabetical) order, not
ranking order, and the \code{sort} query parameter is ignored upstream. The
default \code{max_results = 100} therefore returns the alphabetically-first
100 recruits, not the top-100 ranked recruits. To rank the class, pull a
larger slice and sort the result on \code{overall_rank} (lower is better);
note ESPN only publishes an \code{overall_rank} for the highly-rated portion
of each class, so most recruits carry \code{NA} there. \code{position_rank},
\code{state_rank}, and \code{region_rank} are populated more widely. \code{grade} is
ESPN's 0-100 recruit grade (\code{0} / \code{gradeDisplayValue = "NR"} means not
rated).

\code{committed_team_id} / \code{committed_team_ref} are resolved from the
recruit's \code{schools} array by matching a school whose commitment status
equals the recruit's top-level status (e.g. \code{Signed}); recruits still
uncommitted at fetch time leave both columns \code{NA}.
}
